From: the jellyfin-ffmpeg contributors
Subject: unref the last frame on filter graph cleanup
Origin: https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin-ffmpeg/blob/v8.1.3-2/debian/patches/0106-unref-the-last-frame-on-filter-graph-cleanup.patch

ffmpeg lets go of the frame its frame rate conversion kept when it
rebuilds a filter graph: it belonged to the old graph's frames, which
crashed hwmap.

Rebased onto FFmpeg 9.0.2: the same change, at 9.0.2's line numbers.

From jellyfin-ffmpeg 8.1.3-2, where the patch has no header: its authors
are in that repository's history. Under FFmpeg's licence, as the files
it changes.
---
diff --git a/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c b/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c
--- a/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c
+++ b/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c
@@ -2032,8 +2032,11 @@ static int configure_input_filter(Filter
 
 static void cleanup_filtergraph(FilterGraph *fg, FilterGraphThread *fgt)
 {
-    for (int i = 0; i < fg->nb_outputs; i++)
+    for (int i = 0; i < fg->nb_outputs; i++) {
         fg->outputs[i]->filter = NULL;
+        // drop the last frame retained by FPS conversion before graph reinitialization
+        av_frame_unref(ofp_from_ofilter(fg->outputs[i])->fps.last_frame);
+    }
     for (int i = 0; i < fg->nb_inputs; i++)
         fg->inputs[i]->filter = NULL;
     avfilter_graph_free(&fgt->graph);
